surface station has a published surveyed accuracy, no lunar differential-correction link has a published latency or message format, and the quantization result is a closed form rather than a measurement. RTKLIB — already the external-code oracle for the single-difference and weighted-least-squares kernel — could be driven with a deliberately mis-surveyed base to confirm the 1:1 survey transfer, but that is independent code over the same first-order line-of-sight algebra. MAGNITUDES ARE MODELLED: the survey default is this crate's own lunar frame-realisation allocation rather than a measured station, the latency and bit count are ILLUSTRATIVE inputs, and growth of the broadcast ephemeris error VECTOR itself is not modelled at all — stated in the emitted ageing-law string, because it is the term most likely to dominate a real link</td><td><code>lunar_dpnt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar reference-station coverage and accuracy map</td><td>Monte Carlo sweep of the lunar differential-PNT model over a south-polar user grid and a day of epochs (the <code>lunar-station-coverage</code> kind): for a differential reference station at a known south-polar site, per point and over the region, the percentile corrected and uncorrected position error (3-D, horizontal, vertical), availability with a reason for each unavailable epoch (user_lt4, station_missing_sat), coverage against a threshold stated before the run, DO-229E protection levels, the coverage radius per azimuth sector, and the correction-link budget at the station and at the worst point. Not a statement about the performance of any real system; not affiliated with or endorsed by any agency</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Arithmetic re-computed by an independent numpy script on exported cases (scripts/gen_lunar_station_ref.py, numpy 2.5.4; pre-registered in tests/fixtures/lunar_station/PREREGISTRATION.md, committed alone in 2d43d3d5 before the first run): on 20 cases with every error term on, the visible sets and status are identical, the raw range errors agree to 1.4e-14 m, the position-error vectors and magnitudes to 1.4e-9 m against a 1e-6 m bar, and each point's p95 equals numpy.percentile bit for bit; the station corrections MISSED their 1e-9 m bar (worst 3.73e-9 m, 15 of 20 cases), and with them the corrected range errors: checked in 60-digit arithmetic, both sides are within double-precision rounding of the exact value, the survey term being a difference of two 1e7 m ranges whose last-place unit is 1.86e-9 m; recorded, not tuned. The kind reduces exactly to the lunar-differential-pnt kind at one point, one epoch and one trial. The error model, the decorrelation model and every threshold are this project's own or ILLUSTRATIVE; nothing here is a statement about any real system</td><td><code>lunar_station (over lunar_dpnt, lunar_service)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar terrain horizon masks from a stated profile or a polar DEM</td><td>Per-site azimuth-dependent horizon masks for the lunar-station-coverage kind (the reference station and every user point) and the moonlight-service-volume grid and its per-satellite export site (each export row gains clears_terrain), in the 0.36 skyline table format (skyline::Skyline, linear in azimuth with wrap at 360 deg; a stated profile and a DEM horizon at one site combine as their pointwise maximum): a stated [terrain] station_horizon or user_horizon labelled with its source, and/or a horizon ray-marched over a south polar stereographic DEM (the LRO LOLA gridded polar layout, read from a descriptor that fixes its size and SHA-256, or a latitude/longitude realdata::lola_dem tile with [terrain] dem_tile_path, echoed with its SHA-256) along great circles of the lunar sphere with exact curvature, from a stated mast height, at a stated azimuth step and range. A satellite counts at a site when it clears the flat mask_deg and the horizon at its azimuth (lunar_service::topocentric); an epoch the horizon leaves with fewer than four satellites at a point, where the flat mask gives four, is counted as the unavailable reason terrain_masked. The vendored DEM is a crop of the public-domain LOLA LDEM_75S_240M product, 5 x 5 block-mean resampled to 1200 m (data/lunar-dem/NOTICE.md). Absent, every site keeps the flat mask and every document is byte-identical</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed-form surfaces (InternalConsistency): the bare-sphere dip, a crater in a plateau on the sphere with an observer on and off its centre, and an analytic cone against its own rasterisation. On the real crop, an independent numpy ray-cast (scripts/gen_lunar_terrain_ref.py, numpy 2.5.3; pre-registered in tests/fixtures/lunar_terrain/PREREGISTRATION.md, committed alone in 4fa71559 before the script existed) agrees at five sites: site heights to 1.0e-11 m and all 1800 horizon elevations to 5.5e-10 deg against 1e-6 bars, truncated-ray counts exact; this checks the arithmetic, not the terrain. No published polar horizon or illumination figure has been reproduced: the vendored 1200 m crop smooths the sub-kilometre relief that published illumination studies resolve, so the DEM horizons are a MODELLED line of sight over a resampled real DEM, not a validated horizon. The station site and constellation stay ILLUSTRATIVE; nothing here is a statement about any real system</td><td><code>lunar_terrain (over skyline, lunar_service::topocentric_basis); lunar_station; lunar_service (coverage_masked, masked_visible)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar reference-station multipath and receiver-hardware terms</td><td>The optional [receiver] section of lunar-station-coverage (LRS-02): elevation-dependent multipath sigma(el) = a + b exp(-el / el0) at the user and at the station (the RTCA DO-229 airborne functional form, its a = 0.13 m, b = 0.53 m, el0 = 10 deg as defaults; on the Moon every value is ILLUSTRATIVE), white at the user and first-order Gauss-Markov over epochs at the station (rho = exp(-dt / tau)); early-minus-late DLL thermal code noise sigma = L_c sqrt(B_L d / (2 C/N0) (1 + 2 / ((2 - d) T C/N0))); per-channel inter-channel biases constant over a trial, at the user and at the station. Station terms enter its corrections, user terms the raw and corrected measurements, each on its own seeded stream. Absent, every document is unchanged</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms and statistics of the model's own draws (InternalConsistency): the DLL formula against a hand computation, the DO-229 form, the zero-baseline difference of two receivers, the Gauss-Markov lag-1 correlation. No lunar multipath or receiver measurement exists to compare with; the values are ILLUSTRATIVE and nothing here is a statement about any real receiver</td><td><code>lunar_receiver; lunar_station</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar reference-station ephemeris-error growth over the correction latency</td><td>The optional orbit_err_growth_m_per_s input of lunar-station-coverage (LRS-03): a correction applied latency_s after it was computed misses the growth g latency_s of the broadcast orbit error, g isotropic of 3-D RMS rate, drawn per satellite and trial on its own seeded stream (range 1-sigma rate tau / sqrt(3)), on top of the frozen-error line-of-sight re-projection. Absent, every document is unchanged. A K5 addendum to the published-claims comparison, pre-registered alone, runs K5 at 0.001, 0.01 and 0.1 m/s (ILLUSTRATIVE)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The growth term's closed-form sigma and the first-order propagation check (InternalConsistency). The K5 addendum (tests/fixtures/lunar_station_published/ADDENDUM-LRS03.md, pre-registered alone in f9ce1284): K5 fails at every rate, corrected p95 3-D 369.8 m, 2315 m and 22797 m against a 1.5 m bar (292.96 m recorded without growth), as stated before the run. No public source gives a lunar growth rate: ILLUSTRATIVE</td><td><code>lunar_station (orbit_growth_range_sigma_m)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar reference-station solution modes: height constraint, three satellites, static-user window</td><td>The optional [solution] section of lunar-station-coverage (LRS-06): a height pseudo-measurement row up . dx = e_h of stated 1-sigma weighted against range rows, a three-satellite minimum allowed only with it, and a weighted least-squares solve over the last window_epochs epochs with a shared static position and one clock per epoch (a filter's answer for a static position with no process noise over the same data; the epochs' errors are treated as independent). Absent, the snapshot solve and every document unchanged. An addendum to the published-claims comparison with the sources' solution style, pre-registered alone</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The snapshot reduction and the solver's closed-form cases (InternalConsistency). The addendum (tests/fixtures/lunar_station_published/ADDENDUM-LRS06.md, pre-registered alone in 2b4c34b5) with a 1 m height constraint, three satellites and a 6-epoch window (ILLUSTRATIVE settings; the sources state a filter, a height constraint and three satellites but not their settings): K1 to K5 fail and K6 passes, 1 of 6 as in the recorded run; the R300 corrected p95 3-D error falls from 280.6 m to 2.11 m (horizontal 0.90 m, not scored) and coverage stays 0. The window's independence assumption favours it. Not a statement about any real system</td><td><code>lunar_solution; lunar_station (solution_figures)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar reference-station accuracy and coverage against published claims</td><td>The lunar-station-coverage kind run under the public assumptions of the published material on a lunar differential reference station (four navigation satellites in 24 h frozen elliptical orbits, 10 m 95 % SISE, 0.30 m code noise, 1 s and 600 s correction latency, a sub-decimetre station survey, the published example station site, the 300 km and 600 km south-polar circles) and scored against the sources' numeric claims under six one-sided bars pre-registered before the run: coverage of the whole south pole at sub-metre p95 (K1, K2), sub-metre and decimetre-level corrected p95 (K3, K4), sub-metre kept at 600 s latency (K5), and accuracy kept from 10 km to 500 km baseline (K6)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Reference: the numeric claims of five public sources registered with URL, retrieval date, SHA-256 and licence in tests/fixtures/lunar_station/SOURCES.md (arXiv:2602.08432v3; IOAG Cislunar PNT Workshop 2025 and 2026 slides; UNOOSA ICG WG-B Lunar PNT, June 2024), transcribed with page references in tests/fixtures/lunar_station_published/CLAIMS.md. Bars, cases and inputs were pre-registered in tests/fixtures/lunar_station_published/PREREGISTRATION.md (revision 2, ebac1e14, committed alone before the run). 0.36 first run, a finding (stays MODELLED): 1 of 6 bars passes. Coverage is 0.00 on both circles against &gt;= 0.95, and the corrected p95 3-D error is 280.6 m (300 km), and 293.0 m at 600 s latency, against &lt;= 1.5 m. The decimetre bar (K4) could not pass at the sources' 0.30 m code noise, which reflects a conflict between the sources rather than a model result. K6 passes (ratio 1.39 against &lt;= 1.5), but as a ratio of two geometry-dominated figures it is evidence about decorrelation in neither direction. Cause: the satellites' elements are not public, and on the ILLUSTRATIVE in-tree elements a user and the station share four satellites above 5 deg in only 8.2 % (300 km) and 6.3 % (600 km) of epochs, at near-singular geometry (uncorrected p95 3.8 km against the sources' &lt; 10 m). The sources' simulations used a filter, a height constraint and three satellites in common view, which the snapshot kind does not model. The comparison therefore neither confirms nor refutes the published 'sub-metre over the whole south pole'. It shows that this model's result depends on orbits that are not published. Not a statement about any real system; not affiliated with or endorsed by any agency</td><td><code>lunar_station (over lunar_dpnt, lunar_service)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Lunar ARAIM protection-level kernel</td><td>The lunar protection level's