</td><td>The ground-network overlay: stations with a minimum elevation, the visibility of the vehicle on a stated geometry (the entry state flown two-body with J2, the Moon's position for the cruise, or a stated duty), station handovers that lose a stated time each, and the resulting duty passed to the phase's ground-tracking update rate; the longest interval with no station in view is a budget row</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Zenith and antipode geometry checks and the expected visibility of a distant target in the tests. No real network, antenna or schedule is modelled</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::ground_network</code></td></tr><tr><td>Relative navigation to docking in a lunar orbit (<code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-orbit-rendezvous</code> kind and phase: a joint two-vehicle batch Gauss-Newton estimate over the arc (twenty-one parameters: both states and persistent signal-in-space position biases, common and independent), grown over a quarter, a half and the whole arc with step halving, from an absolute fix per vehicle (AFS pilot code-tracking bound, ILLUSTRATIVE relay set) and a crosslink range, under the crate's lunar force model; the mean normalised estimation error squared, the Clohessy-Wiltshire check on a circular orbit and the 6x6 covariance handed on</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Self-consistency under a pre-registration (xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md, results and the estimator history in RESULTS.md): the mean NEES of the relative state is 5.85 to 5.93 against the band 5.2 to 6.8 in four configurations, MET on the fourth estimator design after three sequential-filter designs missed; the truth and the estimator share one force model, so this says nothing about model error. The propagator it shares is compared with LRO and CAPSTONE in a row below</td><td><code>lunar_rendezvous (LunarRendezvousScenario); lunar_phase; mission_thread::lunar</code></td></tr><tr><td>Landing-dispersion budget at a lunar polar site (<code>lunar-descent</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-descent</code> kind and phase: a five-state per-axis inertial error model (position, velocity, accelerometer bias, tilt, gyro bias) over a powered descent, aided by AFS fixes (standalone or differential, with the clock-aided three-relay case), a terrain-relative fix (the engine's terrain-nav matcher on synthetic relief, or a Cramer-Rao bound on a LOLA DEM with an aperture-problem rule) and lunar time, with a Monte Carlo touchdown ellipse</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The closed-form inertial drift, the reused kinds' own results and the calibration of the 3-sigma ellipse (the fraction inside agrees with its expectation) in the tests. No flown or tested landing is compared</td><td><code>lunar_landing (LunarLandingScenario); lunar_phase; terrain_nav</code></td></tr><tr><td>Surface positioning and timing availability by site and season at the lunar poles (<code>lunar-surface</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-surface</code> kind and phase: relay availability and PDOP, Earth and Sun visibility and a disciplined surface clock for each site and season, with the terrain skyline of <code>moonlight-service-volume</code>; a single-site run equals that kind's availability and PDOP to the bit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Exact equality with the existing kind for one site, and monotonicity checks, in the tests. The relay set is ILLUSTRATIVE and no surface measurement is compared</td><td><code>lunar_surface_map (LunarSurfaceMapScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>Powered ascent from a lunar polar site to an insertion orbit (<code>lunar-ascent</code>)</td><td>The <code>lunar-ascent</code> kind and phase: the five-state per-axis inertial error model run over a powered ascent with specific force along the path, aided by AFS fixes and lunar time, reporting the insertion position and velocity covariance carried to orbit and the engine-cutoff dispersion (reported, not carried)</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The closed-form inertial drift and monotonicity checks in the tests. No flown ascent is compared</td><td><code>lunar_ascent (LunarAscentScenario); lunar_phase</code></td></tr><tr><td>The lunar force model propagation against public reconstructed lunar-orbiter and cislunar trajectories</td><td>The propagator the lunar kinds share (GRAIL GL0660B degree and order 150, Earth and Sun third bodies, relativity, DE440 lunar orientation) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectories of LRO (24 h, 10 minute samples) and CAPSTONE (one 6.5 day revolution, one hour samples), under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons spacecraft ephemerides for LRO and CAPSTONE, against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread-lunar/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendments 1 to 6). LRO: MET, 33 m largest 3-D difference and 2.4 m RMS radial against 500 m and 60 m. CAPSTONE: NOT MET, a 136.8 km end-position difference against 25 km and a 28.4 km periapsis difference against 10 km, the causes not isolated. The row is Modelled because the CAPSTONE bar is missed and the comparison tests the shared propagator, not any filter, signal or budget</td><td><code>lunar_od (LunarForceModel); precise_od; mission_thread_lunar_oracles (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>The cislunar mean-state propagation against the public Artemis I trajectory (<code>tli-and-cruise</code>)</td><td>The mean-state propagation of the <code>tli-and-cruise</code> phase (Cowell, Earth point mass and J2, the Sun and Moon third bodies, the Moon from a DE440 cut) compared with the public JPL Horizons trajectory of Artemis I over a 72 h coast arc from 2022-11-16 18:00 TDB, at 12 hour epochs, under bars fixed in a committed file before the run</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons target -1023 (the NASA navigation as-flown post-TLI trajectory), against bars pre-registered in xval/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION.md (Amendment 1 changes only the request span). NOT MET and asserted as missed: 45.3 km largest position difference against 5 km, and 0.249 m/s velocity difference at 72 h against 0.05 m/s. A diagnostic written after the verdict places the miss in five impulsive velocity steps of 0.09 to 0.22 m/s in the reference (the pre-registered reading that the window held no burn was wrong); over the other 67 hours the model reproduces the next hourly state to a median of 0.38 m. Tests the mean state of one coast arc only: no covariance, filter, update or other phase</td><td><code>mission_thread::earth::tli_cruise; propagator; mission_thread_oracle (example)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</td><td>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and any specific laser's noise are not modelled</td><td><code>inertial::cai_error_terms</code></td></tr><tr><td>Greenhall and Riley exact equivalent degrees of freedom of the Allan, modified Allan and Hadamard variances</td><td>allan::edf_greenhall (with the edf_greenhall_adev, _mdev and _hdev wrappers): the full algorithm of Greenhall and Riley, Proc. 35th PTTI (2003), Section 3 (Eqns 7 to 13 and the large-J coefficients of Tables 1 to 3), for difference orders 1 to 3, noise exponents +2 to -4 wherever alpha + 2d &gt; 1, overlapping and non-overlapping, modified and unmodified estimators; NaN where it does not apply, including the unmodified white-PM case with ceil(M/S) &lt;= d, whose closed form is not implemented. assurance::uncertainty::edf now shares the code and keeps its outputs bit for bit</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>allantools 2024.6 ci.edf_greenhall, a third-party implementation of the published algorithm, run by scripts/gen_greenhall_edf_ref.py over the grid pre-registered in xval/greenhall-edf/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 696f68e6, before any comparison): at all 4 876 points where it returns a finite edf, kshana agrees within a relative 6.0e-13 (bar 1e-9); at its 46 NotImplementedError points kshana returns NaN; every case label occurs; the wrappers equal the core bit for bit; uncertainty::edf is bit-identical over 274 280 values (pinned as a SHA-256 before the refactor). At 32 points (alpha -4, d 3, unmodified, case 2.2) allantools fails in its Table 2 lookup, so they have no oracle value and are not counted. Stable32 was not run. 0.36 revision (docs/revisions/STUDIO15-greenhall-edf-portable.md): on portable maths the kernel takes its logarithm from a double-double evaluation rounded once (allan::greenhall_ln), and the unchanged Bar 1 holds again at all 4 876 points, worst relative 6.0e-13; the uncertainty::edf guard is re-based to the 0.36 portable dump (3 746 of the 274 280 values moved, at most 1.6e-12 relative, from the host-maths base)</td><td><code>allan (edf_greenhall, greenhall_edf_core); assurance::uncertainty (edf)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Oscillator holdover presets from public datasheets</td><td>Four presets — OCXO (Microchip OX-208), rubidium (Microchip 8040C), caesium (Microchip 5071A high-performance tube) and CSAC (Microchip SA.45s) — each carrying its datasheet's Allan-deviation maxima, aging and temperature bound; a non-negative least-squares white + flicker + random-walk frequency-noise fit scaled to envelope every datasheet point, a flicker floor never below the longest-τ figure, linear aging and a sinusoidal temperature term, synthesised as a seeded holdover after a GNSS loss</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The four named Microchip datasheets (document numbers and URLs in each preset and in docs/TELECOM-TIMING.md) for the input figures; the closed-form aging ramp and the fit's envelope property as internal checks. How the figures become a noise model — the fit, the floor rule, the 30-day month, the linear temperature reading — is a modelling choice; no preset is a measurement of a unit. 0.30 round 2, one-sided bars against measured units (pre-registered 43d19980), a finding (stays MODELLED): the IGS station THU2 Symmetricom 8040C in ESA MGEX final clocks has an Allan deviation 0.35 to 0.87 of the rubidium preset but a holdover error 1.07 to 1.60 of it; the 5071A record sits outside the caesium preset to 1000 s (measurement white phase noise, 30x at 1 s) and in holdover (1.15 to 8.74); the worst 2011 production SA.45s is 1.26x the CSAC preset at 1 s; the OX-208 has no measured record (blocked)</td><td><code>telecom_timing (PRESETS, fit_noise, with_flicker_floor, synthesize_holdover); models (ClockModel)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Holdover prediction from a measured clock record, checked on held-out data</td><td>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind's measured-record path: the overlapping Allan deviation of a phase record fitted by edf-weighted non-negative least squares in the white-phase and IEEE Std 1139 frequency-modulation basis (slot_timing::fit_weighted), then inverted for the coast time at which the time error reaches a threshold (slot_timing::slot_budget). The red-noise floor is measured from the record instead of assumed</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>A real free-running atomic clock: 556 990 one-second phase samples of a 5071A caesium standard measured against a hydrogen maser (A. Wallin, distributed with allantools; pinned commit and SHA-256 in scripts/fetch_cs5071a.sh), the same record tests/cs5071a_reference.rs uses for the estimators. The held-out two thirds are an external measurement the prediction never saw. Data-gated: the realdata-clock workflow fetches the record and runs the test with KSHANA_REQUIRE_REALDATA=1. Validates the fit-then-invert path on a white-FM-dominated atomic standard over coasts up to about 50 000 s; not the datasheet or class sources, not the deterministic terms. NOT a crystal oscillator: on a measured OCXO record (tests/slot_timing_ocxo_holdout.rs, allantools, 5.5 h against a hydrogen maser) the held-out prediction is conservative, at 0.59 to 0.70 of the measured breach, and mostly outside the bar, because that oscillator's noise floor halved during the record; in sample it is within 0.98 to 1.17. The crystal case stays MODELLED. NOT atomic clocks in orbit either: on 10 GPS Block IIF satellite clocks (tests/slot_timing_igs_holdout.rs, IGS final 30 s clocks, 14 days, protocol written before download) 8 land within the bar and 2 (G25, G30) are optimistic by up to about 2x, so under the protocol the orbital case stays MODELLED</td><td><code>slot_timing (ClockNoise::from_phase_record, fit_weighted, slot_budget, breach_after_sync_s)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Seconds until a free-running clock leaves a time-indexed slot's guard, and the fix cadence that keeps it inside</td><td>The <code>slot-timing</code> kind: the predicted error E(t) = k·√(σ₀² + σ_x²(t)) + (|y₀| + |c_T·ΔT|)·t + ½|D|t² inverted for the breach, the time left, the resynchronisation interval net of the fix latency and fixes per day, each term itemised with the dominant one named; clocks from the six ClockClass defaults (TCXO, OCXO and RAFS added, each citing one datasheet), the telecom-timing presets, an inline datasheet or a measured record; a breach beyond the source's longest averaging time flagged as extrapolated</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed forms for each term alone and the independent holdover::holdover_seconds root-find; the datasheet figures are the named documents (docs/SLOT-TIMING.md). A class default rests on an assumed red-noise floor and a datasheet source is an envelope of maxima, so neither is a measurement of any unit</td><td><code>slot_timing (predicted_error_s, breach_after_sync_s, terms_at, slot_budget, ClockNoise::from_datasheet, SlotTimingScenario); clock_state (ClockClass::Tcxo, Ocxo, Rafs, source)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Timing protection level for a receiver in orbit under GNSS spoofing</td><td>orbital_timing::orbital_timing, reachable as the <code>slot-timing</code> kind's spoofing section: circular-orbit speed and period, the longest time a ground spoofer can reach the satellite per pass, the clock-aided monitor floor and CUSUM detection latency, the conditional protection level (floor plus coast over the latency), the pull a spoofer at a stated maximum ramp rate accumulates before the satellite leaves its footprint or an independent check runs, and whether each ground-contact or crosslink check is independent of one ground spoofer</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Reduction to the existing timing protection level and the circular-orbit and spherical-Earth visibility closed forms. Conditional on detection, one terrestrial spoofer at a stated ramp rate, an overhead pass on a non-rotating Earth; not validated on a spoofed receiver in orbit</td><td><code>orbital_timing (orbital_timing, visibility_half_angle_rad, max_pass_s, crosslink_neighbour_is_outside_footprint)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Closed-form L-band signal power spectral densities and spectral separation coefficients</td><td>Unit-area power spectral densities of GPS L1 C/A and L2C (BPSK(1)), GPS L5 and Galileo E5a (BPSK(10)), sine-BOC(1,1) and Galileo E1 MBOC(6,1,1/11) (navsignal::Modulation::psd, with an MBOC variant added), their numerically located nulls and maxima (spectrum::psd_nulls_hz, psd_peak_hz, main_lobe_null_to_null_hz), and the spectral separation coefficient of a signal against any spectrum at any offset (navsignal::spectral_separation_coeff_offset) or against a tone, flat noise, a chirp or matched noise (spectrum::Jammer::ssc), with the anti-jam coefficient Q = 1/(R_c kappa)</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Published textbook values: the BPSK(n) main lobe of 2n x 1.023 MHz null to null and the anti-jam coefficients Q = 1 for a narrowband (CW) jammer and Q = 1.5 for a spread-spectrum jammer matched to C/A (Kaplan &amp; Hegarty, Understanding GPS/GNSS, 3rd ed., section 9.4); the BOC(m,n) main lobes centred at plus or minus m x 1.023 MHz (Betz, Binary Offset Carrier Modulations for Radionavigation, NAVIGATION 48(4), 2001); the spectral separation coefficients -61.8, -64.8 and -67.8 dB/Hz for C/A with C/A, BOC(1,1) with BOC(1,1) and C/A with BOC(1,1) (Betz 2001; Hein et al., MBOC: The New Optimized Spreading Modulation Recommended for Galileo L1 OS and GPS L1C, Inside GNSS, May/June 2006), reproduced here from their Parseval autocorrelation closed forms. The BOC(1,1) maximum is not at 1.023 MHz: the lobe spans the carrier null to 2.046 MHz and peaks at 0.759 MHz, and the test pins both. The MBOC mix is the ICD definition, checked for unit area and linearity only</td><td><code>navsignal (Modulation::psd, spectral_separation_coeff_offset, q_from_ssc); spectrum (psd_nulls_hz, psd_peak_hz, main_lobe_null_to_null_hz, Jammer::ssc)</code></td></tr><tr><td>L-band spectrum waterfall with per-band J/S and effective C/N0 under a scripted jammer timeline</td><td>The <code>spectrum</code> kind: a frequency-by-time grid of the L-band power spectral density (thermal floor k T_sys with T_sys = T_ant + 290 K (F - 1), the signals at their interface-specification minimum received powers, and continuous-wave, narrowband, chirp and matched-noise jammers with on/off times), each cell averaged over its bin and row (chirps exactly over whole and partial sweeps, jammers by duty), per-band effective C/N0 = [1/(C/N0) + sum (J/S) kappa]^-1 per row, J/S per band, in-band J/S, and an SVG waterfall with C/N0 bars. The report carries a cross-check against the <code>jamming</code> kind's chain on the same link inputs</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Reduction to the existing <code>jamming</code> kind's anti-jam equation and link budget (the same code, called on the same inputs), and the k T0 F noise-floor closed form. The signal spectra underneath are the validated row above; the jammer powers, timeline and front-end bandwidths are scenario inputs, the spectra are continuous (no spreading-code lines), and no automatic gain control, blanking or antenna pattern acts on the jammer. No measured jammed spectrum is in the repository to check the composite against. The <code>jamming</code> kind's Q table (CW and narrowband 1, broadband and swept 2 since 0.36: the textbook values, pinned to the navsignal closed forms) agrees with this model's CW value and differs from what it derives for noise matched to C/A (1.5) and for flat null-to-null noise without in-band normalisation (2.2); the report prints both</td><td><code>spectrum (SpectrumScenario, SpectrumModel::bin_psd_w_per_hz, SpectrumModel::band_state, effective_cn0_multi_dbhz, system_temp_k, Jammer::power_fraction_in, Jammer::duty)</code></td></tr><tr><td>SigMF recording input and output, and Welch spectral estimates of complex IQ</td><td>sigmf: read and write Signal Metadata Format recordings (JSON .sigmf-meta with the core global, captures and annotations fields; raw .sigmf-data as cf32_le, ci16_le or ci8, the integer decoders shared with realdata::iqif::load_iq), all on strings and byte buffers. spectrum::welch_psd: Hann-windowed, overlapped, averaged periodograms, density-scaled, on an in-crate radix-2 transform (spectrum::fft_in_place). Those two are the validated claim. spectrum::synthesise_iq draws the model as IQ, and the <code>spectrum</code> kind's [iq] section runs model to IQ to SigMF to Welch and compares with the model; its [recording] section estimates a real recording (native builds); the synthesis and those model comparisons are outside the validated claim</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>scipy 1.18.1 scipy.signal.welch (BSD-3-Clause) with the same periodic Hann window, overlap, density scaling, no detrend and two-sided mean average: every bin within 1e-12 relative (observed 6.0e-14) over 27 cases, with segment counts taken from scipy's own spectrogram. sigmf-python 1.13.0 (LGPL-3.0, run as a tool) writes five third-party recordings (cf32_le, ci16_le, ci8, two captures with annotations, a non-zero first sample_start) that the crate decodes bit-identically with identical core fields, and reads the crate's recordings back bit-identically; the SigMF v1.2.6 metadata schema reports zero errors on the crate's metadata. Tolerances fixed before the first comparison. Outside the claim: spectrum::synthesise_iq and the [iq] and [recording] comparisons with the model (a synthesis has no single right answer; a periodic chirp shows lines, Fresnel ripple and edge tails the smooth model omits, total power within 2 %), the integer-to-float scaling convention (the crate divides by 32767 and 127, sigmf-python's autoscale by 2^15 and 2^7, which the specification leaves open; the comparison uses raw codes), multi-channel recordings and the data types the reader refuses</td><td><code>sigmf (read, write, encode, decode, parse_meta, meta_to_json); spectrum (welch_psd, fft_in_place, synthesise_iq)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Planet positions across the solar system from the JPL Standish Keplerian elements</td><td>Heliocentric positions of Mercury, Venus,