ing</code> scenario kind</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Transcription of the named Recommendations (freely downloadable from itu.int), each limit carrying its table or clause, listed with every unconfirmed or unimplemented item in docs/TELECOM-TIMING.md; the breakpoint-continuity identities the tables imply are checked in the tests. A standards transcription with internal consistency checks, not a conformance test and not an external validation. 0.30 round 2 (pre-registered 0f8c3f11), blocked (stays MODELLED): the ten mask values and thirteen ePRTC-A holdover values the Recommendations print as worked numbers (G.8272 Figs. 1-2, G.8272.1 Amd.1 cl. 8.2.1 and Figs. V.1-V.3, G.8271.1 Amd.3 Figs. 7-2 to 7-4) are reproduced inside half a printed unit, most of them table constants or breakpoints; G.8271.1 Fig. 7-2 labels 200 ns at 1.3 s against its own Table 7-1 (197.5 ns). The G.8273.2 class A-D limits have no printed worked value, and the G.8272.1 ePRTC locked breakpoint labels (on solid construction lines) were excluded by the pre-registered selection rule, so those remain transcriptions</td><td><code>telecom_timing (MASKS, eprtc_a_holdover_limit_ns, check_curve, check_mask)</code></td></tr><tr><td>ITU-T frequency-synchronisation wander-generation masks (G.811, G.812, G.8262, G.8262.1)</td><td>Seven constant-temperature MTIE and TDEV wander-generation masks for the <code>telecom-timing</code> kind: G.811 (09/1997) PRC (autonomous), G.812 (06/2004) Types I, II and III, G.8262 (10/2024) EEC Options 1 and 2 and G.8262.1 (11/2025) eEEC (locked mode), including the power-law pieces the tables write (<code>8 τ^0.5</code> and the like), each interval open or closed as printed, measured through the 10 Hz filter the clauses state (taken as applied before sampling); the reference scenario scenarios/telecom-synce-eec.toml sets a free-running OCXO against the three EEC masks. Not implemented: the variable-temperature allowances, holdover, transients, jitter and the G.811 frequency accuracy</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Transcription of the named Recommendations (downloaded from itu.int on 2026-10-13), every row re-read against the text, with the breakpoint continuity the printed coefficients imply bounded in the tests (for example 40 · 10^0.4 = 100.48 ns against the 100 ns plateau of G.812 Table 4). A standards transcription with internal consistency checks, not a conformance test and not an external validation. G.812 (06/2004) is listed as superseded by a pre-published 08/2026 edition and G.811 has an Amd. 1 (04/2016); neither could be read, so neither is checked. The free-running OCXO scenario overstates the oscillator's contribution at observation intervals long against a loop's time constant; it says nothing about any equipment clock</td><td><code>telecom_timing (MASKS, Segment::tau_exponent, MeasurementFilter::LowPass10HzAtSource, MaskCondition::Autonomous)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Clock-log formats (RINEX clock, TimeLab ASCII, TAPR TICC) read to a normalised phase series</td><td>clock_log::read_input: one parser layer that turns a RINEX clock file (AS/AR records of one named clock, versions 2 and 3.00 to 3.04), a TimeLab-style ASCII phase or frequency log, or a TAPR TICC timestamp or interval log into a uniform phase grid, with tau0 stated, from a header, or the median spacing, every gap, off-grid reading and duplicate counted, and a refuse, longest-run or linear gap policy; wired opt-in into the telecom-timing series (<code>[series.clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>clock_log</code> block) and the clock kind (<code>[clock_log]</code>, adding a <code>measured</code> block with ADEV, TDEV and MTIE, the table's SHA-256 and the phase SHA-256; runs = 1). Without the table both kinds are byte-identical</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Pre-registered in tests/fixtures/clock_log/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 05bb1d17, before the fixture existed); recorded in RESULTS.md. Three seeded synthetic logs, one per format (a TICC gap of three epochs, a RINEX second 