g is the body yaw, not the course, and differs from it by up to about 77 degrees on automotive-urban-canyon; GGA quality, satellite count and HDOP are placeholders); whether any laboratory simulator reads the files as intended; any simulator or receiver performance. The oracles check conversions and encodings, not the physics of the scenario. Local-frame handling is checked through the velocity rotation and the NMEA speed and course only (the CSV has no local position column).</td><td><code>interop::testbench (write_motion_csv, write_nmea, trajectory_of, export); fusion::pack::truth_trajectory</code></td></tr><tr><td>Compliance mapping to public frameworks</td><td>compliance (kshana compliance-report): a mapping from Kshana outputs to clauses of public frameworks by number only, filled from the runs given; a result counts for a capability only when it carries the evidence the mapping names, an empty set evidences nothing, and the wording is &quot;supports evidence for&quot;; the report is not a finding that any framework is met and not a rating or approval by anyone.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>References checked against a script-extracted heading index of the cited public documents read 2026-10-10 (tests/compliance_references.rs). WHAT IS CHECKED: 49 cited clause, section, paragraph or heading references exist in the cited document, and 7 cited URLs resolved on the recorded date (2 via a second route); negative controls fail. WHAT IS NOT: that any paraphrase, mapping or gap is correct; EN 16803 clauses (paid, catalogue text only); later revisions; the index is not an independent implementation.</td><td><code>compliance</code></td></tr><tr><td>Marine integrations: Signal K plugin, OpenCPN gate stream and score panel, reference build</td><td>The trust score, band, reasons, gate state and receiver-reported position published under navigation.gnss.kshana in a Signal K server with a warn, alarm and normal notification (integrations/signalk); the gated NMEA stream, with the fix marked invalid while trust is collapsed and a PKSHT sentence per cycle, served to OpenCPN over TCP by the gate itself or by a small relay, and a native OpenCPN score panel that reads the PKSHT sentence (integrations/opencpn); and systemd units and a container that run the gate (deploy/reference-build). Where these show output they carry the advisory statement: Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment (IEC 61108, IEC 61162): the operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>WHAT IS CHECKED, stated narrowly: that these integrations work against the real software they are written for. EVIDENCE is committed at integrations/signalk/evidence/README.md (with its JSON files) and integrations/opencpn/evidence/README.md (with its screenshots and logs), each with the script that reproduces it. (1) Signal K: the plugin loaded into the pinned signalk-server 2.33.0 npm package, in a private loopback-only network namespace, fed synthetic data. The server's own REST and WebSocket interfaces showed the published paths, the score metadata, and a notification that went absent, warn, alarm, normal (after the hold) and warn again when the data stopped. With the real kshana run by the plugin on the NMEA the server itself received, the notification went warn then alarm and the reported position was published; the server's own navigation.position was written by its NMEA provider, never by the plugin. Re-run against live JSON schema 1.2. (2) OpenCPN: the plugin built against the OpenCPN plugin API header and wxWidgets 3.2.4, loaded into the Ubuntu 24.04 package of OpenCPN 5.8.4 under a virtual display; OpenCPN loaded it, ran its initialisation and handed it the PKSHT sentences of the gated stream, and the panel turned red on the untrusted band. In one clean run, OpenCPN's own ship position stopped updating, and its fix indicator dropped, when the gate marked the fix invalid. A second run fed directly by the gate's TCP listener showed delivery to the plugin but not a clean before-and-after. (3) The relay tests replay a recorded excerpt of the gated stream through a real TCP socket and check what a consumer receives (the bytes unchanged, valid checksums on every sentence including the rewritten ones, GGA and RMC agreeing, one PKSHT per cycle with the gate field where the fix is invalid), and the end-to-end script checks that a consumer of the gate's listener receives exactly the bytes the same gate writes to standard output. (4) The PKSHT parsers (JavaScript and C++) are run over sentences recorded from kshana output and reject a wrong checksum, version or a score outside 0 to 100. (5) The systemd units are checked with systemd-analyze verify (systemd 255, binaries stubbed) and with guards for a shell in ExecStart, the restart policy and the sandbox keys; the container entrypoint's argument handling is checked with a stub binary. WHAT IS NOT: there is no external oracle for any value these integrations show. The trust values themselves are the rows above, and an integration run shows only that the plumbing carries them; that is why this row is MODELLED and not VALIDATED (docs/VALIDATION.md). No real vessel, receiver, long run, other version of either host, other operating system or desktop was used; all data are synthetic text and nothing here is a measurement. The notification's sound and visual handling by a real Signal K client, OpenCPN's toolbar button, preferences and stale state, and packaging for the OpenCPN plugin manager were not exercised. The systemd sandbox keys were checked for syntax only, not run under a live systemd, and the container image build and its health check have no committed evidence here. No detection or false-alarm figure is claimed. Advisory software, not type-approved navigation equipment (IEC 61108, IEC 61162): the operator remains responsible for the navigation of the vessel.</td><td><code>receiver_trust (live: the gate, its TCP listener, PKSHT); outside the crate: integrations/signalk (JavaScript), integrations/opencpn (JavaScript relay, C++ native plugin), deploy/reference-build (systemd units, container)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BOC(1,1) tracking: DLL scaling and bump-jump ambiguity resolution (opt-in)</td><td>The boc field of iq::track::LoopConfig (opt-in, None by default): the three DLL discriminators rescaled to read the code error in chips with unit slope on the sine BOC(1,1) main peak, and a bump-jump monitor on very-early and very-late correlators at one subcarrier half-period (0.5 chip) that jumps the code NCO towards the main peak when an up/down counter reaches N (8 by default). Without the option every output is unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency against injected truth and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 700d95df (docs/design/evidence/boc-bump-jump/). A BeiDou B1C data signal (PRN 19, 5 MS/s) generated by iq::scene: with the option off a side-peak start stays false-locked at the predicted ±(0.5 + d/4) chip (measured ±0.549); with it on, one jump towards the main peak at 0.08 s and a late-window RMS of 0.0008 chip at 45 dB-Hz and 0.0048 chip at 35 dB-Hz; no jump from the main peak at 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz over 4 s; the scaled EMLP slope 0.94 to 0.99 on the sampled signal (unscaled 3.6 to 3.8). The bump-jump detector alone, on correlator triples with the exact BOC(1,1) noise covariance, made no false jump in 100 000 updates at 25 to 45 dB-Hz and detected 1000 of 1000 side-peak starts at 35 dB-Hz. NOT MET (finding F1): the pre-registered unit-test grid for the scaling includes offsets outside the discriminators' linear range, where the scaled readings miss the offset by up to 48 %. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: field IQ, band-limited front ends, composite MBOC/QMBOC replicas, and any external receiver.</td><td><code>iq::track::boc (BocKind, BocTracking, BumpJump, boc11_dll_scale); iq::track::channel (the bump-jump taps and Channel::bump_jumps)</code></td></tr><tr><td>IQ ionospheric scintillation channel (Cornell model) against measured 50 Hz GPS L1 scintillation</td><td>iq::channel::scint, the Cornell scintillation model (Rice intensity, second-order Butterworth spectrum, phase arg z) driven at a measured window's S4 and intensity decorrelation time, compared on what it generates beyond those two inputs; realdata::scint50 reads the measured log, detrends it and runs the comparison.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Measured data under bars pre-registered at 653a2195 (xval/scint-malindi/): Zenodo record 16941224 (INGV, Malindi, 18 March 2023, GPS PRN 26, 50 Hz intensity and carrier phase, CC BY 4.0, SHA-256 checked). 22 qualifying 60 s windows (N1 met). MET: the intensity distribution (Kolmogorov-Smirnov D 0.0415, bar 0.05) and the phase scintillation tied to S4 (median measured/model sigma-phi 0.779, bar 2/3 to 3/2). NOT MET (finding F1): the model's deep-fade fraction is 2.38 times the measured (bar 0.5 to 2) and its mean fade 3.51 times as long (bar 2/3 to 3/2). Finding F2: the registered processing reads the model's wrapped phase, which a self-check exposed; B3 repeated post hoc with the phase unwrapped gives 0.804. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other nights, stations, latitudes or signals; receiver noise in the model. The row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::channel::scint (Scintillation, ScintParams); realdata::scint50 (parse_log, segments, measured_windows, model_windows, compare)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Cell-averaging CFAR option in IQ acquisition</td><td>iq::acq::ca_cfar and ca_cfar_cells (opt-in): a cell of an acquisition grid is compared with the mean of reference cells of its Doppler row (guard and stride lags, wrapping round the period), at a factor alpha set from the closed-form CA-CFAR false-alarm probability for M non-coherent blocks (ca_cfar_pfa, ca_cfar_alpha). The sample-power detector stays the default and every existing output is unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Closed form, under bars pre-registered at 8af30331 (docs/design/evidence/acq-cfar/). The CA-CFAR false-alarm probability for Gamma(M) cells, against Monte Carlo of the exact model: 18 of 18 cases within 3 binomial sigma. On every cell of iq::acq searches (GPS L1 C/A, 2.5 MS/s, 210 000 cells each) at a design 1 %: 0.977 % and 0.969 % on Gaussian noise and 1.006 % and 0.999 % on 2-bit quantised noise (M = 1 and 4; bar plus or minus 20 %); decisions unchanged when the input is scaled. NOT MET (finding F1): on a noise-free 2-bit signal the detection and its cell are right, but 1.28 % of the off-peak cells of the peak's row (bar 1 %) exceed the threshold, at the code's deterministic correlation sidelobes. RE-RUN 0.41 (D8a row 3, 1059c34e8): MET on re-run with the opt-in row reference set (CfarConfig::with_reference: reference cells every stride-th lag round the whole Doppler row): 0 of 2495 off-peak cells above on the registered signal and 3, 4, 0 and 0 on four fresh signals (bar 1 %), all detected in the right bin; C2/C3 0.969 to 1.033 % on seen and fresh seeds; the 16-cell window stays the default and its control reproduces 0.40. One candidate tried, on development PRNs 2-6 (disclosed). WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other CFAR variants, two-dimensional windows, band-limited front ends, field data.</td><td><code>iq::acq (CfarConfig, ca_cfar, ca_cfar_cells, ca_cfar_pfa, ca_cfar_alpha)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Spectrum model: GLONASS, BeiDou and Galileo E6 bands (opt-in)</td><td>spectrum::default_band resolves, by name only (MORE_BAND_NAMES), GLONASS L1OF and L2OF on every FDMA channel K = -7 to 6 (BPSK, 0.511 Mchip/s), BeiDou B1I (BPSK(2)), B1C (MBOC(6,1,1/11)), B2a and B3I (BPSK(10)) and Galileo E6 (BPSK(5)), with the carriers and minimum received powers of their interface control documents; the default band set and every existing output are unchanged.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Published interface control documents and closed forms, under bars pre-registered at 502af4a7 (docs/design/evidence/spectrum-bands/): GLONASS ICD Edition 5.1, BDS-SIS-ICD B1I 3.0, B1C 1.0, B2a 1.0 and B3I 1.0, Galileo OS SIS ICD Issue 2.1. MET: every carrier, chip rate and received power as transcribed (28 GLONASS channels), BPSK first nulls at the chip rate, the B1C composite equal to MBOC(6,1,1/11) as its ICD's power table gives, the C/N0 path identity, and byte-identical spectrum scenarios. NOT MET: the unit-area bar, which was unattainable as written (finding F1; the areas equal the closed-form in-band fraction), and the per-bin agreement of the IQ layer's B1I and B1C waveforms with the model envelope (1.26 and 1.56 dB against 0.5 dB, finding F2; GLONASS 0.07 dB), because Gold and Weil codes have uneven line spectra; band-averaged the main lobes agree within 0.25 dB. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: recorded IQ, other signals, intra-system interference.</td><td><code>spectrum (default_band, MORE_BAND_NAMES, GLONASS_CHANNELS)</code></td></tr><tr><td>BeiDou B1I primary ranging codes against an independent generator</td><td>iq::signals::beidou::b1i_bits and b1i: the BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I-3.0 Gold codes truncated to 2046 chips, PRN 1 to 63, as the IQ layer generates, acquires and tracks them.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>GNSS-SDR v0.0.19 (tag v0.0.19, commit 1ddd4562), an independent open-source implementation of the same ICD: its beidou_b1i_code_gen_int, compiled standalone, generated all 63 codes. Only its program output is committed: tests/fixtures/iq_signals/beidou_b1i_gnss_sdr.txt, a 64-chip prefix and the SHA-256 of each 2046-chip code. That output is the BeiDou B1I PRN codes the public BDS-SIS-ICD-B1I-3.0 defines; no GNSS-SDR source or binary is in the repository. Bar pre-registered at db9dd696 (xval/beidou-b1i-gnss-sdr/): all 2046 chips of all 63 PRNs identical, no inversion, shift or relabelling. Result: 63 of 63 identical; the fixture was regenerated from a fresh v0.0.19 checkout (source file SHA-256 as recorded) and is byte-identical. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the NH20 secondary code, the GEO PRN list, the carrier and chip rate.</td><td><code>iq::signals::beidou (b1i_bits, b1i)</code></td></tr><tr><td>GPS L1C ranging and overlay codes against the IS-GPS-800J printed check columns</td><td>iq::signals::gps::l1c_p_bits, l1c_d_bits and l1c_o_bits: the L1CP and L1CD Weil ranging codes (computed Legendre sequence, Weil index, expansion sequence inserted before chip p) and the L1CO S1 overlay codes, PRN 1 to 63.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>IS-GPS-800J (01-AUG-2022, distribution statement A), the check columns it prints beside the generator parameters: Table 3.2-2 initial and final 24 chips of every L1CP and L1CD code, Table 3.2-3 initial and final 11 bits of every L1CO code, Table 6.2-1 the length-10223 Legendre sequence; transcribed by scripts/extract_is_gps_800_tables.py from the PDF (SHA-256 c4aad710...). Bars pre-registered at c6063eb2 (docs/design/evidence/l1c-codes/): 252 of 252 columns, 63 of 63 overlays and 10 223 of 10 223 Legendre bits identical; the extraction --check passes; a negative control fails relabelled, inverted and shifted codes. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the generator parameters themselves (they come from the same tables), the chips between the printed columns, the TMBOC subcarrier pattern and the L1CO S2 codes.</td><td><code>iq::signals::gps (l1c_weil, l1c_ranging_bits, l1c_p_bits, l1c_d_bits, l1co_s1_run, l1c_o_bits); iq::signals::tables</code></td></tr><tr><td>LunaNet AFS-I and AFS-Q primary codes against the LSIS-AFS V1.0 printed check columns</td><td>iq::signals::lunanet::afs_i_bits and afs_q_primary_bits (signal names lunanet-afs-i and lunanet-afs-q in kshana iq): the AFS-I Gold code (2047 short-cycled to 2046, G2 delayed per PRN) and the AFS-Q Weil primary code (computed Legendre sequence, Weil index, expansion sequence inserted before chip p), PRN 1 to 210.</td><td>VALIDATED</td><td>Against LSIS-AFS V1.0 printed tables (marked TBC in the spec): LunaNet Interoperability Specification V5, Applicable Document 1 Volume A: LunaNet Signal-In-Space Recommended Standard, Augmented Forward Signal (LSIS-AFS) V1.0, 29 January 2025 (NASA; PDF SHA-256 986e0795...), the check columns it prints beside the generator parameters: Tables C-1 to C-5 and D-2 to D-6 first and last 24 chips of every AFS-I and AFS-Q code, Table D-1 the length-10223 Legendre sequence; extracted by scripts/extract_lunanet_afs_tables.py (xval/lunanet-afs/oracle/). Bars pre-registered at e9d5e69 (xval/lunanet-afs/PREREGISTRATION.md) before the generator existed: B1 420 of 420, B2 identical, B3 420 of 420. The document marks its code tables {LSIS-TBC} and says they are for test and integration only, so this validates the construction against that edition's tables, not a later one's. A pre-registered claim about the printed G2 initial state (B1b) was stated with the wrong direction and is NOT MET (0 of 210), pinned; the corrected relation (2047 minus the delay) holds 210 of 210 and is recorded as post hoc. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the generator parameters themselves (same tables), the chips between the printed columns, the tertiary code, the message layer and the LDPC coding.</td><td><code>iq::signals::lunanet (afs_i_bits, afs_q_primary_bits, legendre_bits); iq::signals::lunanet_tables; iq::lunanet_afs::check_spec</code></td></tr><tr><td>LunaNet AFS tertiary codes and message layer (SB1 BCH, CRC-24Q, interleaver) against LSIS-AFS V1.0</td><td>iq::signals::lunanet::afs_q_tertiary_bits, afs_q_tiered; iq::lunanet_afs: synchronisation pattern, SB1 BCH(51,8) encoder and 256-hypothesis decoder, CRC-24Q, the 60 x 98 block interleaver, a symbol-stream checker (kshana lunanet-afs-check) and a test-stream generator with placeholder subframes. The rate-1/2 LDPC coding of subframes 2 to 4 is not implemented (its matrices are in a separate annex).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>LSIS-AFS V1.0 (as above): the length-1499 Legendre sequence (Table E-1, identical) and the tertiary first and last 24 chips (Tables E-2 to E-6: 419 of 420 columns identical; the document prints the same last 24 chips, A14850, for PRN 147 and PRN 151 and the generator reproduces PRN 151's, so the PRN 147 entry is most likely a misprint; the bar of 420 of 420 is NOT MET and the miss is pinned); the Figure 8 worked example of the SB1 code (one example: 0x045 encodes to 0x229F61DBB84A0); CRC-24Q against the catalogue check value 0xCDE703 for ASCII 123456789; the document's own frame arithmetic. Bars pre-registered at e9d5e69. One miss on record and one worked example for the message layer, so the row stays MODELLED. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: LDPC and the CRC words inside subframes 2 to 4, message content, and any recorded stream</td><td><code>iq::lunanet_afs; iq::signals::lunanet (afs_q_tertiary_bits, afs_q_overlay_bits, afs_q_tiered); surface (lunanet_afs_check_spec, lunanet_afs_check_symbols, lunanet_afs_generate)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Standards packs: scenarios scored against bars stated before the run</td><td>standards_pack (kshana standards-pack): the DHS Resilient PNT framework test profiles for levels 1 to 4, IMO MSC.1/Circ.1575 accuracy levels and integrity evaluation, and ICAO Doc 9849 Appendix F monitor, assess and mitigate packs: each test runs a bundled scenario kind and compares result fields with bars stated in the pack, and tests that need a receiver log are listed as NEEDS INPUT and not scored.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external for the scenario results: every pack test runs a MODELLED scenario kind. The bars are the pack author's stated profile (the DHS framework states no values; the IMO accuracy limits are Table C-1 of MSC.1/Circ.1575, read in full; the ICAO pack follows ICAO's own RFI Mitigation Plan presentation because Doc 9849 is a paid publication and was not read), committed before any scored run in xval-style PREREGISTRATION.md files under standards/. A MET bar says a modelled scenario met a stated bar, nothing about a product</td><td><code>standards_pack; compliance (standards-pack facts in compliance-report)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory round-trip evidence pack (scenario, test conditions, receiver log, score)</td><td>evidence::roundtrip (kshana evidence round-trip) and the kshana.test-conditions/1 receiver-log export of kshana bench-export: a signed pack that records the scenario hash, the stated conditions' hash, the log hash and the score against the stated events, and refuses conditions that disagree with the scoring events or a log whose hash is not the stated one. No signal is written, replayed or scheduled.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the pack links hashes and a MODELLED receiver-log score, checked on synthetic data (the exported NMEA of a bundled scenario treated as the receiver's log). No laboratory run is claimed</td><td><code>evidence::roundtrip, evidence::assemble, interop::testbench (write_conditions_json), iq::campaign::conditions</code></td></tr><tr><td>IQ tracking engine against GNSS-SDR 0.0.19 on independent recordings</td><td>The full IQ-layer tracking engine (iq::track through kshana iq track: acquisition hand-off, correlators, NCOs, second-order 15 Hz Costas PLL, first-order 2 Hz carrier-aided EML-envelope DLL, lock indicator and C/N0 estimators) on GPS L1 C/A at 30 to 45 dB-Hz.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>GNSS-SDR 0.0.19 (GPL-3.0-or-later, the Ubuntu 24.04 package, run as a separate program; only derived numbers committed) tracking the same numpy recordings (independent of the engine), both measured against the analytic truth. Bars pre-registered at b1f25cce, amended before any test recording at 9496b6b0 (a 10 Hz FLL during pull-in only in both receivers, after the registered calibration failed on a wrong GNSS-SDR hand-off, finding F3). MET at 30, 35, 40 and 45 dB-Hz: carrier jitter within 4.3 %, code jitter within 4.6 % and Doppler mean and spread of GNSS-SDR's. NOT MET: the engine's NWPR C/N0 reads 2.3 to 7.1 dB below GNSS-SDR's SNV (finding F4, the known NWPR limit; its M2M4 is within 0.06 dB of truth); the phase lock indicators differ by definition (F5); at 30 dB-Hz GNSS-SDR slipped twice and the engine not (F6). GNSS-SDR's own hand-off locked to a wrong frequency in 3 of 30 calibration runs. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: other signals, data-bit wipe-off, re-acquisition, field recordings. The row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::track (Channel, LoopCore, design); iq::cli::track</code></td></tr><tr><td>CRPA antenna gains in the jamming kind against an independent numpy recomputation</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [crpa] section: a per-channel beam toward each satellite (null-steering with the jammer as the null, or MVDR on sigma^2 I + P_j a_j a_j^H), scaling each link's noise by ||w||^2 and the jammer by |w^H a_j|^2 before the existing anti-jam C/N0 equation.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent numpy recomputation (xval/crpa-jamming/oracle.py, no shared code: numpy steering vectors, numpy.linalg.solve and inv) of every link of the jamming-demo geometry with a 4-element square array, under both weightings. Bars pre-registered at 716de66a. MET: A1 (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the section, after a fix: the first build added the crpa units to every jamming run's units table), A2 (one element equals no array to 1e-12 dB), A3 on the array noise gain and effective C/N0 (912 link-weightings within 1e-9 dB, worst 2.1e-14 dB) and on the null-steering jammer null (below -200 dB on both sides), A4 (MVDR within 0.1 dB of null-steering at a JNR of 80 dB). NOT MET: A3 on the MVDR jammer gain, 194 of 456 links over 1e-9 dB on 0.36 portable maths (202 on host maths; docs/revisions/STUDIO15-batch4-rebaseline.md) (worst 1.7e-6 dB) on residuals of -100 to -188 dB, where float64 cancellation limits both implementations (numpy's inv and solve disagree with each other on 169 links, worst 7.9e-7 dB); the effective C/N0 these feed is unaffected. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: mutual coupling, element mismatch, wideband processing, multiple jammers, any antenna hardware. A recomputation of the same equations, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming (CrpaCfg, crpa_weights, run_jamming); crpa (null_steering_weights, mvdr_weights, covariance)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Terrain shadowing of the jammer in the jamming kind against an independent numpy recomputation on a real SRTM tile</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [terrain] section: the great-circle terrain profile between jammer and receiver on an SRTM .hgt tile with a k-factor Earth bulge, the dominant knife edge (largest Fresnel-Kirchhoff nu) and the single knife-edge loss of ITU-R P.526-15 section 4.1, subtracted from the jammer's received power.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent numpy recomputation (xval/jamming-terrain/oracle.py, no shared code: its own .hgt read, bilinear interpolation, WGS 84 round trip, profile, bulge, nu and J(nu)) of ten jammer placements around a receiver at Badwater Basin on the committed real SRTM tile tests/fixtures/terrain/N36W117_sub6.hgt. Bars and placements pre-registered at 3b0ebdd4. All MET: additivity (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the section); every placement's profile points and obstacle equal and nu and loss within 1.6e-10 (bar 1e-9); the loss never rises as the jammer rises from 2 to 4902 m; the loss lowers every J/S by exactly itself and nothing else; J(nu) within 1e-12 dB of the oracle's. Findings: placements across the Black and Funeral Mountains lose 44 to 48 dB; two valley-floor placements lose 8 and 15 dB from the Earth bulge alone at 2 m antenna heights, the single knife-edge model's grazing case. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: any measured propagation, multiple-edge diffraction, ground reflection, clutter, the DEM's own error. A recomputation of the same model, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming_terrain (TerrainCfg, jammer_terrain, knife_edge_loss_db); jamming (run_jamming_sky)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Acquisition hysteresis and the front-end quantiser in the jamming kind against independent recomputations</td><td>The jamming kind's optional [acquisition] section (a satellite is acquired at or above an acquisition threshold after a re-acquisition delay and held while at or above the tracking threshold) and [front_end] section (the AGC reading 10 log10(1 + J/N) and the ADC's Gaussian-input quantiser loss taken off every link, flagged as not valid for non-Gaussian jammers).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>An independent Python recomputation of every acquisition flag (xval/jamming-acq-agc/hysteresis.py) from the engine's per-epoch effective C/N0, an independent numpy Monte Carlo of the quantiser loss (xval/jamming-acq-agc/quantiser_mc.py, its own optimum-step search, 2^22 samples), and the IQ layer's AGC loop. Bars pre-registered at 2ca31ee1, with a supplementary run set at 2ef7427a. MET: additivity (all 150 scenario results byte-identical without the sections); every flag equal on the supplementary runs (1824 links, 732 of them showing the hysteresis); monotone in the threshold; the quantiser loss within 0.0007 dB of the Monte Carlo at 1 to 4 bits (bar 0.02 dB); the AGC reading within 0.012 dB of the AGC loop (bar 0.05 dB). NOT MET: the registered hysteresis runs (jamming-demo at 10 to 30 dBW) were uninformative, with every link lost, and their H2 and H4 are met only trivially. RE-RUN 0.41 (D8b row 7, 0ea0da3df): MET on re-run on a 12-power ladder fixed from the scenario file before any engine run (-34 to -21 dBW): 2614 of 5472 link-epochs in the informative 25-32 dB-Hz band (bar 10 %); H2 and H4 hold; all 5472 flags agree with hysteresis.py, 2626 showing the hysteresis; no engine change. Disclosed: the ladder rule was written after supplement 1 had shown where the thresholds lie. Finding: with a CW jammer a 1-bit ADC loses about 5 dB more than the Gaussian figure the engine applies, and flags as not valid. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: the acquisition search itself, AGC dynamics in time, any receiver hardware. Recomputations of the same model, not an external oracle; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>jamming_rx (AcquisitionCfg, AcqState, FrontEndCfg, front_end); jamming (run_jamming_sky); iq::frontend::quant (optimum_step_sigma)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Capped FLL pull-in below the default design's FLL-to-PLL hand-over floor (opt-in)</td><td>iq::track's opt-in carrier.fll_pull_in_max_s (the fll_pull_in_max_s field of iq::track::LoopConfig): under fll_assist = pull-in, the FLL hands over to the PLL after a fixed pull-in time whatever the PLI, with a matching return dwell until the PLI first reaches fll_off_pli.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>Internal consistency against injected truth: a synthetic data-free GPS L1 C/A source (PRN 13, 1500 Hz, 4.1 MHz, 8 s, ten noise seeds) whose carrier phase is known, under bars pre-registered at 8aceabcb (docs/design/evidence/fll-handover-floor/). MET: the control (the uncapped default never hands over in 9 of 10 seeds at 33 dB-Hz); additivity (default hash and the existing tracking tests unchanged); pull-in of a 100 Hz-off hand-off at 45 dB-Hz. NOT MET: with a 1.0 s cap, hand-over and hold in every run (16 of 20 at 33 dB-Hz, 8 of 20 at 31 dB-Hz; the rest cycle between forced hand-overs and the FLL's return) and phase jitter within 25 % of the PLL alone (10.8 against 6.1 degrees at 33 dB-Hz). RE-RUN 0.41 (D8a row 4, 74ba62787): with the opt-in carrier.fll_handover_mean_s (the PLL seeded at the mean NCO Doppler of the pull-in's last 0.5 s), still NOT MET: at 33 dB-Hz hand-over and hold 20 of 20 on seen and fresh seeds with jitter within 1 % of the PLL alone (this half met), but at 31 dB-Hz 15 of 20 seen and 6 of 20 fresh, jitter ratio 1.8 and 2.8. Development seeds 1001-1010 recorded, nothing tuned on them. WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: data bits, other signals and sample rates, field recordings. The default is unchanged; the row stays MODELLED.</td><td><code>iq::track (LoopConfig, FllGate, design); iq::track::channel (the FLL gate)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Write a JPL Horizons state table as a CCSDS OEM, a CCSDS OPM or an SPK kernel, and read it back</td><td>kshana ephem-export (and ephem_export in Python, WebAssembly and MCP): a JPL Horizons vector table is written as a CCSDS OEM 2.0, an OPM 3.0 or an SPK of type 13 or 9 (frame code 1, ICRF). Values and epochs are copied, not computed, and the engine reads the written file back and reports the comparison</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>The engine's own reader on the files it wrote (same codebase). The C7 reference integration has no bar: Orekit 13.1.9 and Basilisk 2.12.2's SPICE read the files back exactly (Orekit's km to m conversion named as rounding further), and ANISE 0.10.6 evaluates the SPK no further from the written states than SPICE does; GMAT is not done until its CI job is green. A one-off check outside the repository found the SPK byte for byte equal to the kernel SPICE's spkw13 writes from the same states. Carries data only; says nothing about any propagation</td><td><code>ephem_export</code></td></tr><tr><td>Optical-navigation measurement model: apparent direction, pinhole projection with distortion, and partials</td><td>opnav::measure: the apparent direction of a body centre (converged Newtonian light time and stellar aberration, the SPICE CN+S correction) or of a catalogue star (aberration only), projected through a pinhole camera with Brown-Conrady distortion in the OpenCV projectPoints convention at a given attitude quaternion, with the partials of the pixel with respect to the spacecraft position and velocity</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>SPICE (spiceypy 8.3.0, CSPICE N0067) and OpenCV 5.0.0, under bars pre-registered in docs/design/evidence/c5a-optical-navigation-model/: O1 body directions within 0.0016 mas of SPICE CN+S (bar 1 mas), O2 stars within 3e-8 mas, O3 projection within 1.3e-11 px of OpenCV, O4 end to end within 3.8e-8 px (bar 1e-4 px), O6 additivity: MET. O5 NOT MET: 258 of 432 partial elements differ from central differences at the registered 1 m and 1 mm/s steps by more than 1e-6 relative (the largest elements agree to about 1e-7 to 1e-9; a no-bar diagnostic at 1 km and 1 m/s cuts the worst relative difference from 1.0 to 8e-4, pointing to difference round-off, not proven). WHAT IS NOT CHECKED: image processing, the body's figure or phase, attitude error, any real camera</td><td><code>opnav</code></td></tr><tr><td>CAPSTONE NRHO propagation with DE440 third bodies and one radiation-pressure parameter</td><td>c3_force::C3Model: the A2 lunar force model with the Sun and Earth from a DE440 kernel plus Venus and the Jupiter system barycentre as point masses (M1), and optionally cannonball radiation pressure k = Cr A/m with Earth and Moon conical shadows (M2). With no kernel and no radiation pressure it is the A2 model bit for bit</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons reconstruction of CAPSTONE (-1176), under bars pre-registered in docs/design/evidence/c3-capstone-nrho-force-model/. C3-R MET on A2's window W0 (21.66 km end position, 4.24 km periapsis; bars 25 and 10 km). C3-H NOT MET on the held-out windows E, H1, H2 (end position 37.25, 36.23, 49.65 km; periapsis met). C3-S NOT MET (k fitted on E: H1 12.92 km met, H2 57.13 km). A2 is not met on re-run. The reconstruction is the reference, not truth</td><td><code>c3_force</code></td></tr><tr><td>Impulsive steps in the cislunar coast and the C2 step detector</td><td>The tli-and-cruise phase's optional impulses list (t_s from the phase start, dv_mps in GCRF) adds each step to the mean state, shortening a step to land on it; without impulses every output is unchanged. mission_thread::impulses::detect flags hours whose one-hour velocity residual exceeds 0.01 m/s and gives one impulse per event hour at its midpoint</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>JPL Horizons reconstruction of Artemis I (-1023), under bars pre-registered in docs/mission-thread/PREREGISTRATION-C2.md: NOT MET on W1 (17.3 km against 2 km; 0.0177 m/s against 0.01 m/s); W2 not run (the published burn times leave 42.8 h, not 72 h). A diagnostic after the verdict shows four of the five steps in the reconstruction carry position jumps no velocity step within one hour can produce. The impulse support itself is checked on synthetic data only</td><td><code>mission_thread::impulses</code></td></tr><tr><td>RTKLIB .pos solution reader for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads an RTKLIB solution file (calendar or GPS week/TOW time, latitude/longitude or ECEF position, satellites used) into the receiver-trust timeline (receiver_trust::machines::rtklib).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: no RTKLIB-produced file was read, and the column layout is from the RTKLIB manual. Checked on self-written files only; the ECEF conversion reuses frames::ecef_to_geodetic, which has its own rows</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::rtklib (read_rtklib_pos); receiver_trust::LogFormat::RtklibPos</code></td></tr><tr><td>NovAtel OEM7 ASCII and binary logs (BESTPOS, RANGE, RXSTATUS) for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads BESTPOS (position), RANGE (C/No per signal) and RXSTATUS (the receiver's jammer flag, as the epoch's self-report) from ASCII and binary OEM7 logs, CRC-checked, read-only (receiver_trust::machines::novatel).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external as a pass bar. The field layouts and CRC follow the public OEM7 documentation (docs.novatel.com); the CRC routine was checked once against the CRC the documentation states for its own BESTPOSA example (cited, not quoted). Fixtures are self-written; no log from a real receiver was read</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::novatel (read_novatel, novatel_crc32); receiver_trust::LogFormat::Novatel</code></td></tr><tr><td>RTCM 3 MSM4 and MSM7 carrier-to-noise reader for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads the C/N0 of every signal cell of RTCM 3 MSM4 and MSM7 messages (GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, SBAS, QZSS, BeiDou, NavIC) from a frame stream, CRC-24Q checked (receiver_trust::machines::rtcm3). Layouts from the BSD-licensed pyrtcm tables, not from the RTCM standard text.</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>ReferenceImpl in a one-off check, not a test in the suite: pyrtcm 1.2.0's decoder reads the same self-written frames to the same C/N0 and epoch time. It shares its tables' source with this reader, so it checks the bit handling, not the layout. No capture from a real receiver or base station was read</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::rtcm3 (read_rtcm3, crc24q); receiver_trust::LogFormat::Rtcm3</code></td></tr><tr><td>GNSS-SDR monitor adapter for receiver-trust</td><td>Reads GNSS-SDR Monitor (Gnss_Synchro) and PVT-monitor messages, relayed to JSON lines with the proto field names, into per-channel C/N0, fix, satellites used, HDOP and sky (receiver_trust::machines::gnss_sdr; integrations/gnss-sdr/relay.py).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: field names come from the GNSS-SDR documentation; neither the reader nor relay.py was run against a live GNSS-SDR or a real capture</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::gnss_sdr (read_gnss_sdr_monitor); receiver_trust::LogFormat::GnssSdr</code></td></tr><tr><td>IMU log ingest, gyro heading input and Allan fit of hybrid sensor parameters</td><td>Reads an IMU CSV (documented columns, or the EuRoC layout); integrates the yaw rate into the heading the heading-course monitor reads ([log.imu]); and fits the accelerometer and gyro noise parameters of the hybrid and fusion scenarios from a stationary log by overlapping Allan deviation (imu-fit).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the fit is checked on synthetic white-noise logs with known coefficients, the heading on constant yaw-rate logs. No real IMU log was fitted, and q_aa is not fitted</td><td><code>receiver_trust::machines::imu, imu_fit; surface::imu_fit; scenario [log.imu]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Home Assistant MQTT discovery and state exporter for trust telemetry</td><td>A JSON state document and Home Assistant MQTT discovery messages for the trust stream, printed as text on every surface and published over MQTT 3.1.1 QoS 0 by the CLI (telemetry::mqtt; trust-telemetry --mqtt-tcp, --print-mqtt, format mqtt).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the packets were read by a loopback listener written for the test, not by a real broker, and Home Assistant was not run</td><td><code>telemetry::mqtt (discovery, state_json, connect_packet, publish_packet, MqttSink); surface::TelemetryFormat::Mqtt</code></td></tr><tr><td>ROS 2 receiver-trust package and gnss_lib_py bridge</td><td>A ROS-free bridge that writes fix and satellite messages as NMEA and scores them through the Python binding (integrations/ros2/kshana_trust), and a gnss_lib_py NavData to receiver-trust bridge through the rtklib-pos format (integrations/gnss_lib_py).</td><td>MODELLED</td><td>None external: the ROS node was not run on a live ROS 2 graph, and the ublox_ubx_msgs field names were not checked against a running driver. gnss_lib_py (MIT) was run for the bridge test</td><td><code>integrations/ros2/kshana_trust (bridge.py, trust_node.py); integrations/gnss_lib_py/kshana_gnss_lib_py.py</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
