e or other phase</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>tests/mission_thread_oracle.rs::the_stored_numbers_miss_the_registered_bars_as_recorded; tests/mission_thread_oracle.rs::the_diagnostic_places_the_miss_in_five_impulsive_events_not_in_the_force_model…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="m" data-g="quantum"><summary><span class="st st-m">Modelled</span><span class="lg-req">Cold-atom interferometer error terms: Raman laser-phase noise, wavefront-curvature bias and fringe-ambiguity resolution</span><code class="lg-mod">inertial::<wbr>cai_<wbr>error_<wbr>terms</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>inertial::cai_error_terms (library functions, not yet reachable from a scenario kind): the Raman-phase weighting function of a square-pulse three-pulse interferometer (Cheinet et al. 2008, Eq. 8) with the white-phase closed form (pi/2)^2 S_phi0/tau_R and a numeric route for any spectrum; the laser frequency noise the retro-reflection delay does not cancel (Le Gouet et al. 2007, Eqs. 9 to 11); the pure-defocus wavefront bias sigma_v^2/R of a thermal cloud and its reduction by a detection radius; the wrong-fringe-index probability erfc(pi/(sqrt(2) s)) for a classical-accelerometer aid and for two interrogation times, and its inverse</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>Each closed form against an independent numerical route in the tests (frequency-domain quadrature of the exact transfer function and delay filter, a Monte Carlo over atoms for the wavefront bias, a Monte Carlo over shots for the fringe index) and its limiting cases. One published worked value: Cheinet et al. 2008 (arXiv:physics/0510197, Sec. IV) state that at tau_R = 20 us a white Raman phase noise below -111 dB rad^2/Hz is needed for 1 mrad per shot; the white term alone gives 0.99 mrad (the paper derives the level with its Eq. 17, flicker plus white). Internal consistency and one worked value, not an external validation; higher-order aberrations and any specific laser&#x27;s noise are not modelled</dd><dt>Tests</dt><dd><code>inertial::cai_error_terms::tests (raman_transfer_short_pulse_limit_matches_the_acceleration_transfer_function; white_raman_phase_closed_form_matches_quadrature; short_pulse_band_limited_closed_form_matches_quadrature…</code></dd></dl></details><details class="lg-row" data-fl-item data-s="v" data-g="clock"><summary><span class="st st-v">Validated</span><span class="lg-req">Greenhall and Riley exact equivalent degrees of freedom of the Allan, modified Allan and Hadamard variances</span><code class="lg-mod">allan <wbr>(edf_<wbr>greenhall,<wbr> <wbr>greenhall_<wbr>edf_<wbr>core); <wbr>assurance::<wbr>uncertainty <wbr>(edf)</code></summary><dl class="lg-body"><dt>Capability</dt><dd>allan::edf_greenhall (with the edf_greenhall_adev, _mdev and _hdev wrappers): the full algorithm of Greenhall and Riley, Proc. 35th PTTI (2003), Section 3 (Eqns 7 to 13 and the large-J coefficients of Tables 1 to 3), for difference orders 1 to 3, noise exponents +2 to -4 wherever alpha + 2d &gt; 1, overlapping and non-overlapping, modified and unmodified estimators; NaN where it does not apply, including the unmodified white-PM case with ceil(M/S) &lt;= d, whose closed form is not implemented. assurance::uncertainty::edf now shares the code and keeps its outputs bit for bit</dd><dt>Oracle</dt><dd>allantools 2024.6 ci.edf_greenhall, a third-party implementation of the published algorithm, run by scripts/gen_greenhall_edf_ref.py over the grid pre-registered in xval/greenhall-edf/PREREGISTRATION.md (committed alone, 696f68e6, before any comparison): at all 4 876 points where it returns a finite edf, kshana agrees within a relative 6.0e-13 (bar 1e-9); at its 46 NotImplementedError points kshana returns NaN; every case label occurs; the wrappers equal the core bit for bit; uncertainty::edf is bit-identical over 274 280 values (pinned as a SHA-256 before the refactor). At 32 points (alpha -4, d 3, unmodified, case 2.2) allantools fails in its Table 2