ing (SPP)","c":"var(--itg)","t":"Can a real receiver position be solved from raw RINEX (Receiver Independent Exchange Format) measurements?","u":"Real-observation single-point positioning (SPP, single-point positioning) from a surveyed IGS station.","b":"A position is solved at 2 of 3 epochs, to 5.74 m (3-D root mean square).","l":"From 9 satellites on average, read from real receiver measurements.","f":[["Epochs total","3","","",0,0],["Epochs solved","2","","",0,0],["Mean n used","9","","",0,0],["Mean PDOP","1.794","","",0,0]]},"time-transfer-budget":{"k":"Clocks & timing · Two-way satellite time transfer","c":"var(--tim)","t":"What do station, transponder, ionosphere and Sagnac terms do to a two-way time offset, and what is its uncertainty budget?","u":"Two-way satellite time and frequency transfer (TWSTFT) between two ILLUSTRATIVE stations, one in central Europe and one in eastern North America, through a geostationary relay at 10 deg W.","b":"The two-way offset is 12.5 ns, with a combined uncertainty of 1.087 ns.","l":"Sagnac 204.4 ns; ionosphere 0.02126 ns.","f":[["Combined uncertainty","1.087","ns","",0,0],["Mean offset","12.5","ns","",0,0],["Half counter difference","-207.2","ns","",0,0],["Ionosphere","0.02126","ns","",0,0]]},"optical-atmosphere-network":{"k":"Clocks & timing · Optical link through the atmosphere, three sites","c":"var(--tim)","t":"How often is an optical timing link up through clouds and turbulence, and what do extra ground sites buy?","u":"Optical PNT (positioning, navigation and timing) through the atmosphere: three ILLUSTRATIVE ground sites (a cloudier northern site, a middling central site and a clearer southern site) viewing a LEO (low Earth orbit) optical terminal at 30 degrees elevation at 1550 nm.","b":"The best single site keeps the optical link up 75 % of the time (cloud-free ceiling 87 %); 3 sites together reach 93 % if their weather is independent, 92 % with the stated correlation.","l":"3 sites modelled; the cloud climates are i